# Python and matplotlib frames from capi_crosscheck.
#
# The test prints "capi crosscheck ok" and then LeakSanitizer reports
# 5734 bytes in 103 allocations. The stacks stay in CPython and in
# matplotlib/_image. A seams, libyodaLib, or dseams frame is not listed.
#
# CPython 3.12 Objects/obmalloc.c, the 1024-byte direct leak.
# These are the allocator frames. A libpython module pattern is not
# listed: it would also match a seams leak reached through the interpreter.
#   #1 _PyMem_RawCalloc
#   #2 PyMem_RawCalloc
#   #3 _PyObject_Calloc
leak:_PyMem_RawCalloc
leak:PyMem_RawCalloc
leak:_PyObject_Calloc
# matplotlib/_image pybind11 records. The module path contains
# matplotlib, which covers:
#   pybind11::cpp_function::make_function_record
#   pybind11::cpp_function::initialize_generic
#   pybind11::detail::process_attribute<pybind11::arg>::init
#   std::vector<pybind11::detail::argument_record>::_M_realloc_append
# pybind11_exec__image is the _image module initializer.
leak:*matplotlib*
leak:pybind11_exec__image
# python_dseams, after the tests print OK. Fast unwinding stops at
# default_calloc, so the PyArray_NewFromDescr suppression never sees
# its frame. A slow unwind of the same 168 bytes / 3 allocations is
# only NumPy and CPython:
#   #1 default_calloc            numpy/_core/_multiarray_umath
#   #2 PyDataMem_UserNEW_ZEROED  numpy/_core/_multiarray_umath
#   #3 PyArray_NewFromDescr_int  numpy/_core/_multiarray_umath
#   #4 PyArray_Zeros_int         numpy/_core/_multiarray_umath
#   #5 array_zeros               numpy/_core/_multiarray_umath
#   #6 _PyEval_EvalFrameDefault  Python/bytecodes.c
# No seams, libyodaLib, or dseams frame.
leak:default_calloc
